BELLMAN–FORD ALGORITHM

(Shortest Path in Graph with Negative Weights)

This one is very important because it solves a case where Dijkstra fails.

1️⃣ Problem Statement

Given a directed weighted graph with V vertices and E edges (weights can be negative):

Find the shortest distance from a source vertex to all other vertices

Detect if the graph contains a negative weight cycle

👉 If a negative cycle exists, shortest path is not possible.

2️⃣ Why Bellman–Ford?
Algorithm	Negative Weights	Detects Negative Cycle
Dijkstra	❌ No	❌ No
Bellman–Ford	✅ Yes	✅ Yes

✅ Used in routing protocols (RIP)
✅ Very common interview theory + coding question

3️⃣ Key Idea (Intuition)

Shortest path in a graph with V vertices can have at most V−1 edges

Relax all edges V−1 times

If any distance can still be improved → negative cycle exists

4️⃣ Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Initialize distance array with ∞

Set distance[source] = 0

Repeat V − 1 times:

For every edge (u → v) with weight w

Relax the edge

Run one more relaxation:

If distance reduces → negative cycle

5️⃣ Relaxation Formula (Core of Algorithm)
if dist[u] + weight < dist[v]
    dist[v] = dist[u] + weight

6️⃣ Java Code (Clean & Interview-Ready)
import java.util.*;

class BellmanFord {

    // Edge structure
    static class Edge {
        int src, dest, weight;

        Edge(int s, int d, int w) {
            src = s;
            dest = d;
            weight = w;
        }
    }

    static void bellmanFord(
            int V,
            List<Edge> edges,
            int source) {

        int[] dist = new int[V];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        dist[source] = 0;

        // Step 1: Relax edges V-1 times
        for (int i = 1; i <= V - 1; i++) {
            for (Edge e : edges) {
                if (dist[e.src] != Integer.MAX_VALUE &&
                    dist[e.src] + e.weight < dist[e.dest]) {
                    dist[e.dest] =
                        dist[e.src] + e.weight;
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Check for negative cycle
        for (Edge e : edges) {
            if (dist[e.src] != Integer.MAX_VALUE &&
                dist[e.src] + e.weight < dist[e.dest]) {

                System.out.println(
                    "Negative weight cycle detected");
                return;
            }
        }

        // Print shortest distances
        System.out.println("Shortest distances:");
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            System.out.println(
                "Vertex " + i + " -> " + dist[i]);
        }
    }
}

